<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    GH-1537: "Compiler use of XMM registers for memset badly recognized"
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/1537

    Source:   char buf[64];  void f(void) { memset(buf, 0, sizeof(buf)); }
    GCC -O2 inlines the memset as one pxor xmm0,xmm0 plus four 128-bit
    movaps stores (RIP-relative to the global).  The decompiler decomposes
    the wide SIMD zero-stores into 64 individual element writes
    (buf[0]='\0'; ... buf[0x3f]='\0';) and never reconstructs the memset.

    Bytes compiled from the issue's exact source (gcc -O2), RIP displacements
    resolved for f@0x100000, buf@0x101000.

    This is the SAME rule decision as GH-9230 (RuleMemsetCopy, constant-fill
    COPY run recovery), exercised here on a GLOBAL destination and RIP-relative
    SIMD stores rather than GH-9230's stack buffer.  No new code beyond GH-9230:
    the fix is pure routing through option memsetrecover (GayHydra #477, ported
    from kuna's gh1537-simd-memset).  Fork default is off (stock output); kuna
    ships it on, so kuna's script toggled it off for its second pass and this
    script toggles it on instead.

    Pass 1 (default, off): assertion #1 reproduces the bug (element stores).
    Pass 2 (memsetrecover on): assertion #2 proves the single memset.  The fork
    prints a space after each call argument comma (kuna prints none).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">
f30f1efa660fefc00f2905f10f00000f
2905fa0f00000f2905031000000f2905
0c100000c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern void f(void);</com>
  <com>map address r0x101000 char buf[64]</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option memsetrecover on</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="GH1537 #1: default (memsetrecover off) keeps the element-store form (stock behavior)" min="1" max="1">buf\[0x3f\] = '\\0';</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="GH1537 #2: memsetrecover on reconstructs the single memset (routing via the GH-9230 rule)" min="1" max="1">builtin_memset\(buf, 0, 0x40\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
